		{
			"id":"56daba4da4e420fe",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD)",
			"styleAttributes":{"textAlign":"center"},
			"x":-152,
			"y":560,
			"width":237,
			"height":140,
			"color":"6"
		},
		{
			"id":"8ed0c9f961248432",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"- **Irritability is best conceptualized as a mood construct**, defined as increased proneness to anger relative to developmental peers.\n    \n- **Severe irritability consists of two core components:**  \n    (1) frequent temper outbursts (phasic) and  \n    (2) persistent irritable mood between outbursts (tonic)\n    \n- **Chronic irritability is distinct from bipolar disorder**, as it lacks episodic mood shifts and shows different longitudinal and familial patterns\n    \n- **Irritability strongly predicts later depression and anxiety**, functioning as a developmental risk pathway\n    \n- **Irritability is transdiagnostic and appears across multiple disorders (ODD, ADHD, depression, anxiety, ASD)**, suggesting it reflects shared underlying mechanisms rather than a single disorder",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":480,
			"y":1670,
			"width":440,
			"height":550,
			"color":"4"
		},
		{
			"id":"b4db60e8ff229804",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"### **Tonic Irritability**\n\n- Persistent, baseline irritable or angry mood\n    \n- Present most of the day, most days\n    \n- Not dependent on a specific trigger\n    \n- Reflects a mood state between outbursts\n    \n\n**For Example:** “Always on edge” or chronically grouchy.",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-428,
			"y":1040,
			"width":628,
			"height":240
		},
		{"id":"5a1555c189556c8b","type":"file","file":"Images/all_classes_mean_trajectories.png","x":-460,"y":2399,"width":1080,"height":756},
